{"context":"sto:verify-changed-gate (M3a) was planned as 'run the checks a standard declares for this repository' (acceptance: 'The pre-commit and pre-push hook templates call it'). Recon before implementation found two problems. (1) No declared-checks mechanism exists anywhere: eka-standard and eka-core carry no way for a std-/spec- artifact to declare a command, so stage (a) had nothing to run — that mechanism is itself sto:standard-check-declarations (M3b). M3a was therefore ordered before its own input. (2) The hook templates live in eka-standard/templates/hooks/{pre-commit,pre-push}, are embedded into the eka-cli binary via standardembed.Hooks(), and are pinned by digest in eka-cli/vendored.yaml. Editing a hook template is therefore not a CLI-only change: it requires a new standard release and a re-pin of eka-cli (and eka-mcp). Standard 1.4 is frozen at d213b99 (tag-immutable), so a hook template change must ride standard 1.5 — the same release sto:knowledge-path-binding (M1) inherits.","decision":"M3a is delivered in two stages and its scope is narrowed to what has teeth today. Stage A (this wave, zero specification change) lands `eka verify --changed` running a built-in, repository-scope check set — not declared checks — plus CI wiring. The built-in set: (i) AGENTS.md managed-region drift, reusing bootstrap.BuildAgentsMDBlock over the committed snapshot (the M0 gate); (ii) committed-snapshot integrity, reusing exchange.LoadSnapshot so a corrupt or partial-aggregate snapshot fails; (iii) conformance bebas terhadap eka-core/conformance, scoped to changed authoring files when a docs/ tree exists. Each check maps to a constraint id and prints the exact `eka get` command for it; blocking violations exit 1, warnings never do. Stage B (rides standard 1.5, alongside M1) installs the check call into the pre-commit/pre-push hook templates, non-blocking by default so the capture contract is preserved. M3a remains a single work item; it stays in progress across both stages rather than being marked done after stage A.","consequences":"Positive: `eka verify --changed` is usable and CI-wired immediately with no specification change; each stage is independently reversible; the M0 drift gate gets a first consumer. Negative: M3a stays open across a release boundary; the built-in check set is a temporary construct that M3b will generalize (declared checks) and may later subsume check (iii). Risk: the built-in set must not be mistaken for the declared mechanism — M3b is still required, and a standard that declares no checks must remain honestly checklist-only.","alternativesConsidered":"(a) CLI ships its own hook templates independent of the standard — rejected: two hook sources is block duplication and they drift. (b) Cut standard 1.5 early to carry the hook change ahead of M1 — rejected: forces a release to land a single shell line, and entangles two unrelated changes in one release. (c) Wait for M3b before starting M3a at all — rejected: leaves the verify surface unbuilt while the M0 drift gate already produces a check worth wiring. (d) Mark M3a done after stage A with its hook acceptance silently dropped — rejected: a work item that claims completion it did not achieve is how the earlier standard-version defect sat unnoticed."}